{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96738.pdf|21", "text": "Advanced Air Sealing \u2014 Measure Summary\n\nor a click on the link, misses it. Two row labels were broken the same way ('Applicabili ty', 'Project Configurat ion File(s)') and are joined too. The cell text is otherwise the extractor's own. -->\n\n| Measure Title | Advanced Air Sealing |\n|---|---|\n| Measure Definition | This measure reduces infiltration rates in existing buildings with baseline infiltration levels of 6 ACH50 (air changes per hour at 50 pascals) or higher. |\n| Applicability | Buildings with infiltration rates \u22656 ACH50 are eligible for an upgrade to 5 ACH50. While some regions require tighter envelopes for new constructions, 5 ACH50 reflects the average infiltration rate achieved in empirical studies across representative climate zones and ensures that selected homes do not already comply with 2024 IECC requirements. Table 4 provides additional details. With these applicability constraints, the measure upgrade applies to 92.2% of the existing dwelling units. |\n| Not Applicable | This measure does not apply to new construction nor to existing units with infiltration rates below 6 ACH50. These constraints exclude 7.8% of the dwelling units from receiving this measure upgrade. |\n| Original Release | 2025 Release 1: 2025/resstock_amy2018_release_1; 2025/resstock_amy2012_release_1; 2025/resstock_tmy3_release_1 |\n| Project Configuration File(s) | https://github.com/NatLabRockies/resstock/blob/sdr_2025_Release_1/project_national/sdr_upgrades_amy2018.yml |", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96738.pdf", "doc_title": "Advanced Air Sealing", "section": "Measure Summary", "line_start": 211, "line_end": 217, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "11", "measure_name": "Advanced Air Sealing", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 2025 Release 1", "date_last_updated": "2026-10-29", "date_last_updated_source": "pdf_moddate"}}
{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96738.pdf|22", "text": "Advanced Air Sealing \u2014 1 Technology Summary\n\nAir sealing is designed to reduce unwanted air leakage through a home's building envelope by sealing gaps and cracks. This helps prevent conditioned indoor air from escaping, and outdoor air, moisture, dust, and pollutants from entering. The result is lower energy use for heating and cooling equipment, and often improved indoor comfort, better indoor air quality, and building durability. This section provides an overview of common air sealing methods and references applicable building codes that set air leakage targets by climate zone. These specifications inform the air leakage rate included in this measure upgrade.", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96738.pdf", "doc_title": "Advanced Air Sealing", "section": "1 Technology Summary", "line_start": 221, "line_end": 221, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "11", "measure_name": "Advanced Air Sealing", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 2025 Release 1", "date_last_updated": "2026-10-29", "date_last_updated_source": "pdf_moddate"}}
{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96738.pdf|23", "text": "Advanced Air Sealing \u2014 1.1  Introduction to Technology\n\nAir sealing is a method used to reduce the amount of unwanted air that leaks in and out of a building through small gaps, cracks, and openings in the walls, floors, ceilings, and around windows and doors. These leaks allow outside air to enter the home and conditioned air to escape, which can lead to higher energy bills, drafts, uneven temperatures, indoor air quality concerns, and moisture issues [3], [4]. Studies show that air leakage can account for up to 30% of a home's heating and cooling costs [5]. By sealing these gaps, air sealing helps lower energy use, improves comfort by reducing drafts, supports better indoor air quality by keeping pollutants and allergens out, and protects the building's structure by reducing moisture intrusion [4]. Common air sealing techniques include: weatherstripping around doors and operable windows; caulking around stationary components such as window frames, baseboards, and wall penetrations; foam sealants or spray foam for larger gaps around plumbing penetrations, recessed lighting, and attic hatches; and airtight electrical boxes or gaskets around outlets and switches [3], [6]. In new construction, house wrap (a breathable material installed under exterior siding) is often used to limit air infiltration while allowing moisture to escape. In most climates, vapor retarders may be installed to manage indoor moisture and prevent condensation w", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96738.pdf", "doc_title": "Advanced Air Sealing", "section": "1.1  Introduction to Technology", "line_start": 225, "line_end": 225, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "11", "measure_name": "Advanced Air Sealing", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 